# KASAN debug kernel — a diagnostic, never a shipping config.
#
#   KERNEL_CONFIG=kasan build/build.sh kernel   ->  build/out/h713-kernel-kasan.fit
#
# Built for one open problem: mpv playing hardware-decoded video on the panel
# takes the board down in roughly half of runs, with a NULL dereference whose
# workqueue work function is 0x0 and a PC that symbolizes to an unrelated
# built-in, followed by a cedrus timeout and RCU stalls. Those are signatures of
# corrupted kernel memory, and KASAN names that class of bug in one run instead
# of a week of bisecting. Hardware decode alone is stable, and the display path
# alone is stable, so the fault is in the combination — see docs/vaapi-scope.md.

CONFIG_KASAN=y
CONFIG_KASAN_GENERIC=y
# OUTLINE calls the check rather than inlining it: a much smaller Image than
# INLINE for a slower kernel. This board decodes 1080p in 0.6 s of CPU, so it
# has the headroom, and the Image has to cross a 10.8 KB/s serial line.
CONFIG_KASAN_OUTLINE=y
# vmalloc coverage matters here specifically: module text and DRM/GEM
# allocations live there, and a driver overrunning a vmalloc allocation is
# exactly the shape of bug being hunted.
CONFIG_KASAN_VMALLOC=y

# Because this session has now spent two physical power cycles on a wedged
# board with no way in. Even if a hard lockup outruns it, sysrq-b is free.
CONFIG_MAGIC_SYSRQ=y

# The four drivers in play during the crash, built IN rather than as modules.
# Two reasons, and the second is not optional:
#
#   1. KASAN only instruments code compiled in this tree. As modules they would
#      be the one part of the kernel NOT checked -- which is precisely backwards,
#      since they are the suspects.
#   2. A module built without KASAN cannot safely be loaded into a kernel built
#      with it. KASAN changes the layout of core structures, so a stale .ko
#      would read the wrong offsets and corrupt memory itself -- and this
#      investigation is about memory corruption.
#
# The defconfig has good reasons for these being modules (a bad load can be
# undone without reflashing a kernel). That safety is preserved differently
# here: the production FIT is left untouched and this one is loaded by name from
# the U-Boot prompt, so the way back is a normal boot.
CONFIG_VIDEO_SUNXI_CEDRUS=y
CONFIG_DRM_SUN50I_H713_AFBD=y
CONFIG_DRM_PANFROST=y
CONFIG_SUNXI_SCANOUT_DMABUF=y

# uname -r becomes 6.18.38-kasan, so /lib/modules/6.18.38-kasan does not exist
# and udev loads nothing. That is the point: it makes rule 2 above impossible to
# violate by accident, and it leaves the production kernel's module tree intact
# on the same rootfs. (The out-of-tree aic8800 WiFi/BT modules therefore do not
# load under this kernel. Nothing in this investigation needs them, and the
# console is serial.)
CONFIG_LOCALVERSION="-kasan"
